        "titolo_orig": "Real Madrid vs Bayern Munich: Champions League – team news, start, lineups",
        "testo_it": "Real Madrid e Bayern Monaco si sfidano a Madrid per l'andata dei quarti di finale di Champions League.",
        "priorita": "Bassa",
        "id_gruppo": 8,
        "link": "https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2026/4/6/real-madrid-vs-bayern-munich-champions-league-team-news-start?traffic_source=rss",
        "orario": "13:11",
        "timestamp": 1775473884,
        "contenuto_esteso": "By Al Jazeera Staff and AFP Published On 6 Apr 2026 6 Apr 2026 Who: Real Madrid vs Bayern Munich What: Champions League quarterfinal, first leg Where: Santiago Bernabeu, Madrid, Spain When: Tuesday, April 7 at 9pm (19:00 GMT) How to follow: We’ll have all the build-up on Al Jazeera Sport from 16:00 GMT in advance of our live text commentary stream. Vincent Kompany’s Bayern Munich visit Real Madrid high on confidence and looking like Europe’s most complete side, bolstered by the likely return of superstar striker Harry Kane. Recommended Stories list of 4 items list 1 of 4 ‘Arnold No.1’: Iraq coach given hero’s welcome after sealing World Cup spot list 2 of 4 Iran’s World Cup participation depends on relocation from US, minister says list 3 of 4 Messi scores as Inter Miami open new stadium with a draw list 4 of 4 Haaland grabs hat-trick as Man City thrash Liverpool to reach FA Cup semis end of list Kane, who has scored 48 goals in 40 appearances across all competitions this season, missed Bayern’s battling 3-2 Bundesliga win at Freiburg on Saturday with an ankle issue. “He’d play in a wheelchair,” said Bayern midfielder Joshua Kimmich, while Kompany added that he had “a feeling” that the England striker would be ready to face Madrid. Despite Madrid’s prestige and troupe of attacking stars – Kylian Mbappe, Vinicius Junior and Jude Bellingham included, as well as City hat-trick hero Federico Valverde – Bayern are arguably favourites to lift the trophy. Record 15-time winners Madrid have endured a difficult season, plagued by inconsistency, but have made a habit of pulling off the unexpected in this competition. Just as it seemed their well of Champions League magic was running dry, Madrid produced their best performance of the season to crush Pep Guardiola’s Manchester City in the last 16. ‘We feel unbeatable at the moment’ With Michael Olise and Luis Diaz joining Kane in attack, Kompany’s side play an aggressive, high-pressing style which helped them demolish Atalanta in the last 16, 10-2 on aggregate. Advertisement Bayern came from two goals down to beat Freiburg at the weekend and match-winner Lennart Karl said that fighting spirit was crucial heading into the Madrid clash. “It gives us a lot of confidence. It was very important – we actually feel unbeatable at the moment,” he said, with Bayern’s last defeat coming in January, 14 matches ago. In that same period Madrid have lost four times, with coach Alvaro Arbeloa struggling to find the right balance when all his superstars are fit, just like Xabi Alonso and Carlo Ancelotti before him. “They’re not playing the best football, but they’re outstanding in terms of experience,” noted honorary Bayern President Uli Hoeness. “It would be presumptuous to assume that we’ll definitely advance,” he added. “But we haven’t had such great chances in terms of the playing quality as we have this year in a long time.” ‘I know what my players are capable of’ Madrid fell to a disappointing defeat at Mallorca in La Liga on Saturday, leaving them seven points behind rivals Barcelona in the Spanish title race. Arbeloa brushed off the defeat and said it would have no bearing on his team’s performance against Bayern, in the club’s favoured competition. “I know what my players are capable of, I know they understand the importance of Tuesday’s game,” Arbeloa told reporters. “The demand on the team will be maximum and I know the support that Madrid fans will offer us – with them, everything is easier.” Bernabeu demands ‘nerves of steel’ Bayern are well aware of the impact of Madrid’s supporters on big European nights at the Bernabeu, which, along with the weight of history and sublime individual quality, helps Los Blancos pull off remarkable results even when the odds are against them. “It’s the stadium and the fans who, together with the team, transform into a hurricane that sweeps over the opponent,” said Bayern powerbroker Karl-Heinz Rummenigge. “You need nerves of steel, you have to stay calm, and you have to be mentally prepared … it’s not just an ordinary game. It will be the toughest test.” Two Bayern players who understand that more than most are goalkeepers Manuel Neuer and Sven Ulreich, who have both cracked in the Bernabeu pressure cooker before. In the 2018 semifinal Ulreich went to pick up a back pass before suddenly realising his mistake and let it run past him, with Karim Benzema tapping into the empty net. Advertisement Madrid went on to win that tie, as they have on all of the last four knockouts against Bayern. In each of those years, they went on to lift the trophy. In the 2024 semifinals former Stoke City striker Joselu struck twice in the dying moments of the second leg, the first after a Neuer handling error, to send Bayern crashing out. No teams have faced each other more in the competition, with Tuesday’s match the 29th occasion Madrid will meet Bayern. Each club has 12 wins over the other, with four of their encounters ending as draws. The German giants last eliminated Los Blancos in 2012 but believe they finally have the quality this year to rewrite the narrative of the last decade. Last 10 encounters: May 8, 2024: Real Madrid 2-1 Bayern (Champions League semifinal) April 30, 2024: Bayern 2-2 Real Madrid (Champions League semifinal) May 01, 2018: Real Madrid 2-2 Bayern (Champions League semifinal) April 25, 2018: Bayern 1-2 Real Madrid (Champions League semifinal) April 18, 2017: Real Madrid 4-2 Bayern (Champions League quarterfinal) April 12, 2017: Bayern 1-2 Real Madrid (Champions League quarterfinal) April 29, 2014: Bayern 0-4 Real Madrid (Champions League semifinal) April 23, 2014: Real Madrid 1-0 Bayern (Champions League semifinal) April 25, 2012: Real Madrid 2-1 Bayern(p) (Champions League semifinal) April 17, 2012: Bayern 2-1 Real Madrid (Champions League semifinal) Madrid’s team news First-choice goalkeeper Thibaut Courtois is Real Madrid’s biggest absentee as a thigh injury picked up during the last 16 second-leg win over Man City has ruled him out for about six weeks. Andriy Lunin is set to play between the sticks again in Courtois’s absence. Rodrygo is also sidelined with an injury, while Ferland Mendy and Dani Ceballos are doubts. However, key midfielder Federico Valverde is set to return to the side after serving a suspension in La Liga. Jude Bellingham may have to be content with starting on the bench as he works his way back towards full fitness following a hamstring injury. Predicted lineup: Lunin (goalkeeper); Alexander-Arnold, Rudiger, Huijsen, Carreras; Valverde, Tchouameni, Pitarch, Guler; Mbappe, Vinicius Jr. Bayern’s team news Ulreich, Cassiano Kiala, David Santos and Wisdom Mike have all been ruled out with injuries, while Kane will face a late fitness assessment. If Kane is unable to play, then Nicolas Jackson is expected to lead the line. Olise and Kimmich are set to return from serving suspensions in the last 16. Predicted lineup: Neuer (goalkeeper); Stanisic, Upamecano, Tah, Laimer; Kimmich, Goretzka; Olise, Gnabry, Diaz; Kane."
